{"schema":"clawhub.skill.verify.v1","ok":false,"decision":"fail","reasons":["security.status_not_clean"],"slug":"mx-stocks-screener","displayName":"Intelligent Stocks Screener","pageUrl":"https://clawhub.ai/financial-ai-analyst/skills/mx-stocks-screener","publisherHandle":"financial-ai-analyst","publisherDisplayName":"Financial AI Analyst","publisherProfileUrl":"https://clawhub.ai/financial-ai-analyst","version":"1.0.15","resolvedFrom":"version","tag":null,"createdAt":1776424764495,"card":{"available":true,"path":"skill-card.md","url":"https://wry-manatee-359.convex.site/api/v1/skills/mx-stocks-screener/card?ownerHandle=financial-ai-analyst&version=1.0.15","sha256":"624ac3650fb7f8b1ea59731a093cd5d5d1246e8bef212bdd462ba48c62080218","size":2341,"contentType":"text/markdown; charset=utf-8"},"artifact":{"sourceFingerprint":"a7f9a085609041892be0996302919556e8e8928c8d44209c87bfdad024e7c8cd","bundleFingerprints":["3f26b1e7e8eeb5ffe84952aff8f463f82f2b1567d88827b077963435ed25693f","6915653803374618d6247a96a77e50a47e6b933daba6601e756e7eb6dd621dde"],"files":[{"path":"SKILL.md","size":5511,"sha256":"e138343f46be0d253a5d289548354207f87c50d765ce3955743cabb7bc05ad9e","contentType":"text/markdown"},{"path":"scripts/get_data.py","size":17162,"sha256":"e46df6fdd014eb465fc26fe40537d8416a3a12296cf26c5c510983e8e8034bdd","contentType":"text/plain"}]},"provenance":{"source":"unavailable","reason":"No server-resolved GitHub import provenance is stored for this version."},"security":{"status":"suspicious","passed":false,"rawStatus":"suspicious","verdict":"suspicious","confidence":"medium","summary":"This is a coherent Eastmoney stock-screening skill, but its CSV export can preserve untrusted spreadsheet formulas from remote data, which users should review before installing.","model":null,"checkedAt":1789172483626,"scannerReports":{"aig":{"version":"2.1.0","$schema":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/oasis-tcs/sarif-spec/main/Schemata/sarif-schema-2.1.0.json","runs":[{"tool":{"driver":{"name":"aig-skill-scan","version":"0.2.1","informationUri":"https://github.com/Tencent/AI-Infra-Guard","rules":[{"id":"T01","name":"Skill Instruction Hijacking","shortDescription":{"text":"Alters the agent's current session goals or safety constraints when the skill is loaded"}},{"id":"T02","name":"Agent Memory Poisoning","shortDescription":{"text":"Writes attacker-controlled rules into persistent memory so they continue to affect future sessions"}},{"id":"T03","name":"Remote Payload Retrieval and Execution","shortDescription":{"text":"Fetches code from an external URL, allowing the effective payload to change after skill review"}},{"id":"T04","name":"Embedded Malicious Code","shortDescription":{"text":"Ships malicious scripts inside the skill package and executes them locally when invoked"}},{"id":"T05","name":"Unauthorized Access and Privilege Escalation","shortDescription":{"text":"Breaks least-privilege boundaries by obtaining permissions beyond the task's legitimate needs"}},{"id":"T06","name":"System Persistence","shortDescription":{"text":"Installs cross-session backdoors, hooks, services, or scheduled tasks that survive the skill run"}},{"id":"T07","name":"Tool Hijacking and Spoofing","shortDescription":{"text":"Modifies, wraps, spoofs, or replaces tools so legitimate-looking calls execute attacker logic"}},{"id":"T08","name":"Insecure Dependencies","shortDescription":{"text":"Introduces malicious packages or components through dependency confusion, typosquatting, or unsafe sources"}},{"id":"T09","name":"Insecure Skill Coding Practices","shortDescription":{"text":"Exposes exploitable flaws such as hardcoded secrets, command injection, plaintext sensitive data, or unsafe temp files"}},{"id":"other","name":"Other Risk","shortDescription":{"text":"Security issues not covered by the T01-T09 classification"}}]}},"results":[{"ruleId":"T09","level":"warning","message":{"text":"CSV Formula Injection in Exported Screening Results"},"locations":[{"physicalLocation":{"artifactLocation":{"uri":"scripts/get_data.py"},"region":{"startLine":320,"endLine":323}}}],"partialFingerprints":{"primaryLocationLineHash":"3d6cb14ac6c7bb04"},"properties":{"description":"## Vulnerability Details\n  **File Location**: `scripts/get_data.py`, lines 320-323  \n  **Vulnerability Type**: CSV formula injection caused by unsanitized remote data  \n  **Risk Level**: Medium\n\n  ### Vulnerable Code\n\n  ```python\n  with open(csv_path, \"w\", newline=\"\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as f:\n      writer = csv.DictWriter(f, fieldnames=fieldnames, extrasaction=\"ignore\")\n      writer.writeheader()\n      for row in rows:\n          writer.writerow(row)\n  ```\n\n  ### Technical Analysis\n\n  The application writes column names and values derived from the remote MCP response directly to a CSV file. It does not neutralize strings beginning with spreadsheet formula indicators such as `=`, `+`, `-`, or `@`. Leading tab or carriage-return characters can also be used to conceal formula prefixes from simplistic validation.\n\n  CSV quoting performed by `csv.DictWriter` does not prevent Microsoft Excel or another spreadsheet application from interpreting a cell as a formula. The documentation explicitly states that generated CSV files can be opened with Excel, making spreadsheet evaluation part of the expected usage.\n\n  An attacker able to influence the East Money API response, including through compromise of the remote service or its upstream data, could return a value such as a formula that initiates an external request when the CSV is opened. The same issue applies to remotely derived column headers because `writer.writeheader()` writes them without sanitization.\n\n  ### Attack Path\n\n  1. An attacker gains the ability to influence a value or column name returned by the configured MCP endpoint.\n  2. The attacker supplies content beginning with a spreadsheet formula prefix.\n  3. `_datalist_to_rows` converts the value to a string without neutralizing formula syntax.\n  4. `csv.DictWriter` writes the attacker-controlled content unchanged into the generated CSV file.\n  5. The user opens the CSV file in Excel or another formula-evaluating spreadsheet application.\n  6. The spreadsheet interprets the cell as a formula.\n  7. Depending on the spreadsheet application, version, security settings, and formula used, the formula may trigger external network requests, disclose data included in the spreadsheet, or invoke unsafe legacy spreadsheet functionality.\n\n  ### Impact Assessment\n\n  Exploitation requires control over, or compromise of, data returned by the remote MCP service and subsequent opening of the generated file in a spreadsheet application.\n\n  Successful exploitation operates in the context of the user opening the CSV. The most credible impact is an attacker-controlled external request that can disclose spreadsheet data or identify the user and environment. More severe outcomes depend on legacy spreadsheet features or insecure local spreadsheet configuration and are not guaranteed by the code alone. The flaw does not directly grant application or operating-system privileges before the CSV is opened.","severity":"Medium"},"fixes":[{"description":{"text":"## Remediation Suggestions\n\n  Sanitize every remotely derived CSV value and header before writing it:\n\n  1. Treat values as potentially dangerous if, after applicable normalization, they begin with `=`, `+`, `-`, `@`, tab, or carriage return.\n  2. Prefix dangerous values with an apostrophe or another spreadsheet-compatible text marker.\n  3. Apply the same protection to `fieldnames` before calling `writeheader()`.\n  4. Preserve the original untrusted value separately only if required; do not place it directly in formula-evaluating cells.\n  5. Consider generating a spreadsheet format that supports explicit text cell types rather than relying on CSV.\n  6. Document that exported data is untrusted and should not be opened with formula execution enabled.\n  7. Add tests covering malicious headers and values beginning with `=`, `+`, `-`, `@`, tab, and carriage return.\n  8. Test values containing leading whitespace or control characters to ensure normalization cannot bypass the protection.\n\n  A centralized sanitizer should be applied immediately before CSV serialization so that both `dataList` and `partialResults` output paths receive identical protection."}}]},{"ruleId":"T08","level":"note","message":{"text":"Unpinned Third-Party Dependency Installation"},"locations":[{"physicalLocation":{"artifactLocation":{"uri":"SKILL.md"},"region":{"startLine":97,"endLine":97}}}],"partialFingerprints":{"primaryLocationLineHash":"413f0103a5835eba"},"properties":{"description":"## Vulnerability Details\n  **File Location**: `SKILL.md`, line 97  \n  **Vulnerability Type**: Unpinned dependency and missing integrity verification  \n  **Risk Level**: Low\n\n  ### Vulnerable Code\n\n  ```bash\n  pip3 install httpx --user\n  ```\n\n  ### Technical Analysis\n\n  The installation instruction retrieves the latest version of `httpx` accepted by the package resolver without specifying a reviewed version or verifying an integrity hash. Consequently, two installations performed at different times may resolve to different package versions and dependency trees.\n\n  The package name corresponds to the legitimate `httpx` project, and the audit found no evidence of typosquatting, dependency confusion, or an intentionally malicious dependency. The concern is that an unpinned installation unnecessarily expands exposure to future compromised releases, malicious transitive dependencies, or newly introduced vulnerabilities.\n\n  The `--user` option limits installation to the invoking user's package environment but still permits installed package code to execute with that user's privileges when imported or during package installation processes.\n\n  ### Attack Path\n\n  1. A package release or transitive dependency available from the configured Python package index is compromised or contains malicious code.\n  2. A user follows the documented unpinned installation command.\n  3. The package resolver selects the affected release because no approved version or hash is enforced.\n  4. Installation-time behavior, or later import and execution by `scripts/get_data.py`, runs the affected package code.\n  5. The malicious code executes with the permissions of the user running the installation or Skill.\n\n  This path depends on compromise or malicious publication in the dependency supply chain; the reviewed project itself does not provide evidence that the current `httpx` package is malicious.\n\n  ### Impact Assessment\n\n  If the dependency supply chain were compromised, package code could access files, environment variables, and network resources available to the invoking user. This could include access to `EM_API_KEY` when the script imports and uses the dependency. The code would not automatically gain administrative privileges, and the scope would ordinarily remain limited to the current user's permissions.","severity":"Low"},"fixes":[{"description":{"text":"## Remediation Suggestions\n\n  1. Pin `httpx` and its transitive dependencies to reviewed versions.\n  2. Maintain dependencies in a lock file generated by a trusted dependency-management tool.\n  3. Require package hashes during installation, such as with a hash-locked requirements file and `pip install --require-hashes`.\n  4. Review and update pinned versions through a controlled dependency-update process.\n  5. Run vulnerability and provenance checks against each proposed update.\n  6. Use an explicitly trusted package index rather than inheriting arbitrary index configuration where operationally possible.\n  7. Perform installation in an isolated virtual environment instead of the general user package environment."}}]}],"properties":{"securityScore":65,"primaryLanguage":"Markdown","llm":"gpt-5.6","startTime":1789172392.7532456,"endTime":1789172453.419175}}]},"skillspector":{"skill":{"name":"mx-stocks-screener","source":"/tmp/clawhub-codex-scan-v57av3va963s2md8dse6410m0s8e797w-YpsrQH/clawscan-skillspector-151492610/artifact","scanned_at":"2026-09-12T00:19:51.985465+00:00"},"risk_assessment":{"score":24,"severity":"MEDIUM","recommendation":"CAUTION","max_issue_severity":"MEDIUM"},"components":[{"path":"SKILL.md","type":"markdown","lines":169,"executable":false,"size_bytes":5511,"source_url":null,"source_identity":null,"source_digest":null},{"path":"scripts/get_data.py","type":"python","lines":433,"executable":true,"size_bytes":17162,"source_url":null,"source_identity":null,"source_digest":null}],"structured_summaries":[],"issues":[{"id":"LP3","finding_id":"finding-eb5ad8f612354647903874ccf764d2e6","category":"MCP Least Privilege","pattern":null,"severity":"MEDIUM","confidence":0.93,"location":{"file":"SKILL.md","start_line":1,"end_line":null},"finding":null,"explanation":"The skill requires an API key, performs network access, and writes output files, but does not declare any explicit tool scope or permission boundaries. This weakens least-privilege controls because a host may grant broader capabilities than users expect, increasing the risk of unauthorized network use, secret handling mistakes, or unintended file writes.","remediation":"Add explicit permission metadata such as allowed tools/permissions for network, environment-variable access limited to EM_API_KEY, and file-write access restricted to a specific output directory. Document the external domain allowlist (for example the Eastmoney API endpoint) and constrain filesystem writes to the declared output path.","code_snippet":null,"intent":null,"tags":["ASI02"],"evidence":{},"match_fingerprint":null,"occurrences":[{"file":"SKILL.md","start_line":1,"end_line":null}]},{"id":"SQP-3","finding_id":"finding-2cec289d7f1844c3960a017f625521ee","category":null,"pattern":null,"severity":"MEDIUM","confidence":0.97,"location":{"file":"scripts/get_data.py","start_line":2,"end_line":5},"finding":null,"explanation":"The module docstring states the tool converts output to Chinese column names and describes the skill entirely in Chinese, which communicates a fixed language behavior. There is no indication that users can opt into another language or that the Chinese-only output is required for a documented region-specific purpose.","remediation":"Document the locale restriction and why it is required, or add an option to preserve original column names / choose output language based on user preference.","code_snippet":null,"intent":null,"tags":["llm-unconfirmed"],"evidence":{},"match_fingerprint":null,"occurrences":[{"file":"scripts/get_data.py","start_line":2,"end_line":5}]},{"id":"SQP-3","finding_id":"finding-c4bafb817c27488b84b91867f0690969","category":null,"pattern":null,"severity":"LOW","confidence":0.88,"location":{"file":"SKILL.md","start_line":3,"end_line":3},"finding":null,"explanation":"The main description is presented in both Chinese and English inline, and the rest of the document is primarily Chinese, but the skill does not state whether users can choose their preferred language or locale. Under the policy for natural-language issues, forcing or assuming a language without explicit user opt-in can be a locale-policy concern.","remediation":"Document supported languages explicitly and state that the skill will respond in the user's preferred language, or provide a clear opt-in/selection mechanism for Chinese vs. English output.","code_snippet":null,"intent":null,"tags":["llm-unconfirmed"],"evidence":{},"match_fingerprint":null,"occurrences":[{"file":"SKILL.md","start_line":3,"end_line":3}]}],"suppressed_count":0,"suppressed":[],"metadata":{"has_executable_scripts":true,"skillspector_version":"2.11.2","llm_requested":true,"llm_available":true,"meta_analysis_applied":true,"inference_usage":[{"node":"mcp_tool_poisoning","request_kind":"structured_output","provider":"openai","model":"gpt-5.4-2026-03-05","model_source":"provider_response","usage